entry transmutation
term: Nuclear Transmutation
difficulty: advanced
image: ../../assets/images/terms/Transmutation.webp
related_terms: decay
related_terms: nuclear-fission
related_terms: isotope
related_terms: ernest-rutherford
related_terms: glenn-seaborg
quick_facts: Alchemy's goal, achieved via nuclear physics (Rutherford, 1919)
quick_facts: Decay chains are nature's own slow transmutation
quick_facts: Elements beyond iron were born in stars and supernovae
quick_facts: Reactors transmute U-238 into plutonium-239 routinely
quick_facts: Proposed as a way to shrink long-lived nuclear waste

sources:
    {"organization":"IAEA","title":"Nuclear Transmutation","url":"https://www.iaea.org/"}

definition:
    Changing one element into another by altering the number of protons in its nucleus — through radioactive decay or deliberate bombardment. Rutherford achieved the first artificial transmutation in 1919 (nitrogen into oxygen), fulfilling the alchemists' dream by physics rather than philosophy. Every element heavier than iron was forged by transmutation inside stars or supernovae, reactors breed plutonium from uranium-238 by it, and proposed 'partitioning and transmutation' schemes would blast long-lived waste isotopes into shorter-lived ones.

debunking_snippet:
    “Chemistry can change one element into another.” No chemical process can — chemistry only rearranges electrons. Changing elements requires changing the nucleus itself.
